Q: CSS adaptive to JS condition A: <p>A cleaner way is to let React switch the class name based on the weather status, instead of writing multiple ternary conditions.</p>
<pre><code>const weatherClass = {
  clearDay: &quot;clear-day&quot;,
  cloudy: &quot;cloudy-day&quot;,
  rainy: &quot;rainy-day&quot;,
  night: &quot;night&quot;,
};

return (
  &lt;p className={`example-element ${weatherClass[weatherStatus]}`}&gt;
    Weather content
  &lt;/p&gt;
);
</code></pre>
<p>Then handle the styles in CSS:</p>
<pre><code>.example-element {
  background-size: cover;
  transition: 0.3s ease;
}

.clear-day {
  background-image: url(&quot;/images/clear.jpg&quot;);
  background-color: blue;
  color: white;
}

.cloudy-day {
  background-image: url(&quot;/images/cloudy.jpg&quot;);
  background-color: gray;
  color: white;
}

.rainy-day {
  background-image: url(&quot;/images/rain.jpg&quot;);
  background-color: #555;
  color: white;
}

.night {
  background-image: url(&quot;/images/night.jpg&quot;);
  background-color: #111;
  color: white;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This keeps the logic simple: React decides <strong>which weather class to use</strong>, and CSS controls <strong>how each weather condition looks</strong>. Adding a new weather type only requires adding one more entry to the <code>weatherClass</code> object.</p>

Q: How to avoid repeated LoadWith methods in complex queries A: <p>These are the helper extension methods I wrote to do what you're talking about (placed in <code>public static class Linq2dbExtensions</code> or something like that):</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// requires most or all of these depending on .NET version:
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Linq.Expressions;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using LinqToDB;

/// &lt;summary&gt;
/// Go from standard syntax (which requires repeating the LoadWith statement):
/// 
/// db.Posts
///   .LoadWith(p =&gt; p.PostComments).ThenLoad(o =&gt; o.CreatedUser)
///   .LoadWith(p =&gt; p.PostComments).ThenLoad(o =&gt; o.UpdatedUser);
///   
/// To this:
/// 
/// db.Posts.LoadThen(p =&gt; p.PostComments,
///   o =&gt; o.CreatedUser,
///   o =&gt; o.UpdatedUser
/// );
/// 
/// Behind the scenes, we are just calling LoadWith, and ThenLoad in a chain.
/// &lt;/summary&gt;
public static ILoadWithQueryable&lt;TEntity, IEnumerable&lt;TProperty&gt;&gt; LoadThen&lt;TEntity, TProperty&gt;(
    this IQueryable&lt;TEntity&gt; source,
    Expression&lt;Func&lt;TEntity, IEnumerable&lt;TProperty&gt;&gt;&gt; selector,
    params Expression&lt;Func&lt;TProperty, object&gt;&gt;[] childrenSelectors
) where TEntity : class
{
    if (source == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(source));
    if (selector == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(selector));
    if (childrenSelectors?.Any() != true) return source.LoadWith(selector);
    ILoadWithQueryable&lt;TEntity, object&gt; currentSource = null;
    foreach (var childSelector in childrenSelectors)
        currentSource = (currentSource ?? source)
            .LoadWith(selector)
            .ThenLoad(childSelector);

    return currentSource.LoadWith(selector); // the final LoadWith is redundant but satisfies the return type
}

// if you need the overload with a separate loadFunc:

/// &lt;inheritdoc cref=&quot;LoadThen{TEntity, TProperty}(IQueryable{TEntity}, Expression{Func{TEntity, IEnumerable{TProperty}}}, Expression{Func{TProperty, object}}[])&quot;/&gt;
public static ILoadWithQueryable&lt;TEntity, TProperty&gt; LoadThen&lt;TEntity, TProperty&gt;(
    this IQueryable&lt;TEntity&gt; source,
    Expression&lt;Func&lt;TEntity, IEnumerable&lt;TProperty&gt;&gt;&gt; selector,
    Expression&lt;Func&lt;IQueryable&lt;TProperty&gt;, IQueryable&lt;TProperty&gt;&gt;&gt; loadFunc,
    params Expression&lt;Func&lt;TProperty, object&gt;&gt;[] childrenSelectors
) where TEntity : class
{
    if (source == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(source));
    if (selector == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(selector));
    if (loadFunc == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(loadFunc));
    if (childrenSelectors?.Any() != true)
        return source.LoadWith(selector, loadFunc);
    ILoadWithQueryable&lt;TEntity, object&gt; currentSource = null;
    foreach (var childSelector in childrenSelectors)
        currentSource = (currentSource ?? source)
            .LoadWith(selector, loadFunc)
            .ThenLoad(childSelector);

    return currentSource.LoadWith(selector, loadFunc);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that this is just syntactical sugar and the question of whether you <em>should</em> use an extension method like this is very much up for debate, but if code repetition is as nails-on-chalkboard unbearable for you as it is for me, perhaps this will soothe your mind.</p>

Q: Bullmq get a job with a specific ID from a queue A: <p>A common pattern is to have a single worker that implements different logic depending on a job type:</p>
<pre><code>const worker = new Worker(
  'queueName',
  async job =&gt; {
    switch (job.name) {
      case 'task1': ...
      case 'task2': ...
    }
  }
);
</code></pre>
<p>When you just listen on the queue events instead of running a worker on a queue, no lock is obtained when you retrieve the job. That's why you cannot modify the job and get the <code>Missing lock for job</code> error.</p>
<p>You could fetch jobs manually with <code>getNextJob</code>, too. Not sure why it didn't work for you. But the above is a simpler approach.</p>
